p = "tests/README.md"
s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
old = s[s.index("| `test_radar_encoding.py`"):s.index("| `test_object_head.py`")]
new = ("| `test_radar_encoding.py` | information preservation of the radar encoder, levels A-C: A unit tests with known answers (rasters exact where they claim), B ceiling of the representation on real nuScenes mini (held-out scenes): strict = own slot, represented = some stored entry of the cell within all tolerances; C (`--learned [steps]`) the shipped abs codec, learned encoder + 1x1 decoder from the default start: plain, tolerance-weighted, C = 128, skip, held-out vs train | A exact; C: tolerance-weighted >= 0.9 x oracle ceiling on held-out, gap <= 3 points | A passes; B (0.5 m): strict simple 41.0 %, slots K=3 87.7 % (K=6 97.3 %), represented K=3 96.6 %; oracle decode 95.0 %; C FAILS as documented baseline: plain 24.9 %, tolerance-weighted C=64 26.2 %, C=128 43.1 %, skip 49.4 % (train = held-out: underfitting; the remedy is `radar_codecs.py`) |\n"
       "| `radar_codecs.py` | levels D-F of the same question: which CODEC of the stored fields and which probe start keep more. Codecs abs / delta (predictor + residual against the ray origin) / phys (r, psi derived from position and origin) / Fourier; oracle decode must equal abs; probes: default vs identity start, ReLU vs LeakyReLU, C = 64 / 128, tolerances x 1 / x 0.25, two seeds, held-out vs train (`[steps] [e \| f]`; plan.md section 3.4) | codecs lossless to 0.5 points (the gate); learned results are reported | D (default start, tol x 1, oracle 95.0 %): abs 25.3 %, delta 70.6 %, phys 67.1 %, Fourier 12.8 %; C = 128: abs 42.8 %, delta 84.4 %, phys 85.7 %. E (identity start, C = 64): abs 91.0 % (ReLU) / 94.1 % (leaky), delta 94.5 / 94.4 %. F (tol x 0.25, oracle 88.6 %, leaky + identity): abs 81.2 / 75.9 % (two seeds), delta 86.0 / 86.4 %, phys 86.6 %, Fourier 66.2 %; train - held-out +4 points = the oracle's own (+3.8), so overfitting is about 0.5 point |\n")
s = s.replace(old, new)
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)
print("ok")
